storage: evict a cached game whole or not at all, never its sets alone

evict_cache_older_than deleted every api_cache row older than sixty days
except the login rows and the User-Agent row. The hourly refresh upserts a
game's patch, unlocks and startsession rows -- so those never age -- and
touches neither its achievementsets row (keyed by hash, written once by a
launch or a bulk cache) nor its gameid alias. So, sixty days after a game
was cached, exactly the row a RetroArch 1.22 launch loads the game through
was gone (handle_offline_request serves achievementsets from that row and
its gameid fallback and rebuilds nothing from patch), while the refreshed
patch row kept the game in cached_game_ids.txt and in every "cached" count
(es_export.collect_cached_game_ids, rom_browser.list_cached_games). The
game was still counted as cached and no longer playable offline: a
RetroArch without a connection read "Load failed (-27): no cached
response" (ROCKNIX audit #186 PL-02, High). On a casual cadence -- a game
scanned once and not opened for two months -- that is the ordinary case,
and nothing said so.

Eviction is now by game: a stale row is kept while any patch or
achievementsets row of its game is younger than the cut-off, and a game's
rows go together when all of them are stale (the login rows and the
User-Agent row are kept as before). The game a row belongs to is read from
its key for patch, unlocks and startsession, and from its body for
achievementsets (GameId) and gameid (GameID). Nothing is loaded unless a
stale row exists at all, so the hourly pass over a healthy store costs one
SELECT of keys and ages.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue maxengel/rocknix#186, PL-02); its
raofflineproxy-ctl now also treats a game whose achievementsets row is
missing as not cached, so a bulk cache re-adds one lost before this patch.
Offered upstream as-is (ROCKNIX D-RA-016).

--- a/linux/raofflineproxy/storage.py
+++ b/linux/raofflineproxy/storage.py
